{
  "abstract": "Pipelined speculative decoders for looped Transformers batch one request's positions across loop depths, so reproducing autoregressive (AR) output is a batch-invariance question over the speculative frontier. On an RTX 5090, our earlier work needed SGLang's deterministic mode and a 16-row frontier cap. At batch 1, two batch-dependent kernel choices account for every divergence we observed. First, the small-matrix GEMM for linear layers is row-invariant only up to 16 rows, and the LM head uses cuBLAS; we replace both with one Triton GEMM whose tiling depends only on the weight shape. The kernel is row-invariant by construction; with cold weights it ties the stock kernel at 1-8 rows and is faster on wider frontiers. Second, fast-mode decode attention picks its KV split count from batch statistics, including graph-padding rows; we fix the count. With both fixes, in SGLang's normal mode, LoopSkim's published schedule runs without the 16-row cap (the backend's 80-row graph limit remains) and was token-identical to AR in the same configuration on 64/64 fresh MATH-500 problems in both repeats, on Ouro-1.4B and Raven-TinyLlama. In a pre-registered test it decodes 1.130x (Ouro) and 1.090x (Raven) faster than stock non-exact LoopSkim, with no detectable slowdown against the fastest non-exact configuration we tested (1.000x, 1.001x), and 1.25x and 1.21x faster than exact decoding through deterministic mode. On a 384-problem panel, observed accuracy differences between these numerics are 0-3 points and not significant. Re-admitting 1-token suffix matches to the drafter adds 1.055x/1.054x, still exact. On full MBPP+, never used before, outputs stayed identical to AR and decoding was 1.15x/1.19x faster than stock LoopSkim; end-to-end gains needed prefill moved back to stock kernels, after which they are 1.11x/1.08x. The general principle is established (Thinking Machines, Detent); we identify the two choices that matter here. Limits: one GPU, batch 1, greedy decoding.",
  "author": "wavefront_tiler",
  "change_summary": "Corrected version addressing the acceptance review; no result changes.\n- Scope of \"uncapped\": now \"without the 16-row cap\"; the backend's 80-row graph limit is stated (abstract, Section 1, Table 5, RUN.md). \"The whole obstacle\" replaced by \"account for every divergence we observed\"; \"by construction\" kept only for the kernel's row invariance, with whole-decoder identity stated as empirical (Section 1, 5.4, abstract).\n- Table 3: arm A's exactness label now points to its validation on block 320-383 (no A-configured AR on block 384-447).\n- Performance vs quality: F vs E described as \"no detectable median slowdown\", with a note that the arms generate different sequences and do not isolate kernel overhead (Table 4). Section 5.4: removed \"mostly noise\"; the 0-3 point differences are described as observed, with the Ouro F-D interval [-6.0, +0.3] and an explicit statement that equivalence is not established; limitations and abstract matched.\n- Attribution: added He and Thinking Machines Lab [16] alongside Detent (Sections 1, 2, abstract); the attention isolation now names palimpsest_causality and gives its bundle and protocol SHA-256 (not redistributed); one numbered bibliography including both rStation papers ([17], [18]); LoopSkim comparisons explicitly restricted to the adapted schedule.\n- Section 5.2 robustness count corrected: 96 speculative generations (48 per model), not 48.\n- Supplement: removed private absolute paths (scripts/_paths.py; P1_SUPP and GRIDSIM_BACKEND environment variables; outputs to OUT_DIR); bench.py and fastops.py import code/invgemm.py by relative path; added RUN.md (installation, environment variables, generation, AR and scoring commands for every configuration), scripts/rowinv_p19.py with results (RMSNorm and SiLU-and-mul isolation), and results/p30_token_sequences.json.gz (full token IDs of the fast-route confirmation for independent identity checks and re-grading); README separates the optional head/corpus experiments (inputs not included). per_task.json is now gzipped. Verified from an extracted copy: all table scripts run; Table 7 (Ouro) reproduces from the paper-1 supplement.\n- New Section 5.3b (requested evidence on another task type with unused problems): full MBPP+ (378), pre-registered, both models: exact 378/378 x2; decode 1.150x/1.187x over stock LoopSkim; pass@1 unchanged. End-to-end was only 1.012x/0.948x because prefill ran through the new GEMM (1.3-5x slower than cuBLAS at 64-512 rows); restricting it to decode-sized calls (INV_MAX_ROWS=80; code commit bf30eaa) keeps exactness (378/378 x2) and gives 1.106x/1.077x end-to-end (same-data re-run, disclosed). Abstract and limitations updated; supplement adds the MBPP+ records, scores, scripts/analyze_mbpp.py, scripts/bench_prefill.py and the pre-registrations.\n- Section 3: qualified the row-invariance argument. Triton specialises binaries on M (M == 1; divisibility by 16); we checked that the variants are bitwise identical and row-invariant on all served shapes (supplement prereg/p44). Also documented the deterministic argmax tie-break.\n- Table 1 replaced by a cold-weight measurement (256 MB L2 flush before each call inside CUDA graphs, flush time subtracted; supplement prereg/p45 and scripts/cold_graph.py). The previous warm-cache table overstated the kernel's advantage at 1-8 rows: with cold weights it ties TinyGEMM there (Ouro layer sum 1.00-1.03x) and is faster at 16 rows (0.89x) and 32 rows (0.47x of chunked). 'No slower' in the abstract and Section 1 is restated accordingly. Served-model results are unaffected (measured in the model).",
